+// Coalesces region preparation - see
+// fix_reduce_wx_mediation_cost.py's docstring. Device logs showed
+// regions being prepared 64KB at a time, one debugger round trip
+// each, continuing for the life of the process. The trap is initiated
+// here in the target, so the debugger cannot skip it; the only way to
+// reduce the cost is to stop asking. One 1MB chunk covers what
+// previously took sixteen traps, and once a process's working set is
+// covered the traps stop entirely.
+//
+// 1MB chunks across a reserved region of at most a few hundred MB fit
+// comfortably in a 64-bit mask. Addresses outside the reserved range
+// bypass the cache and prepare exactly what was asked for, and a chunk
+// is only marked prepared AFTER the debugger call returns.
+// 4MB chunks across 64 mask bits gives 256MB of coverage. At the
+// previous 1MB the ceiling was 64MB, which Ion blew straight past:
+// 917 of its 1031 region requests bypassed the cache entirely and
+// were prepared uncoalesced at 64KB, an 8.6% coalescing rate. Ion
+// with JIT off used 57MB and so coalesced 100%.
+//
+// That also explains why raising javascript.options.ion.threshold
+// appeared to help - it was not improving Ion's compilation, it was
+// reducing Ion's allocation count so it stayed inside an undersized
+// cache. See fix_chunk_coverage_and_dead_connection_detach.py.
+//
+// SIZED TO THE REGION, not to one word. The mask used to be a single
+// uint64_t, so it described the first kMaxPrepareChunks = 64 chunks and
+// nothing else - 4MB x 64 = 256MB, against a MaxCodeBytesPerProcess of
+// 2044MB on this build. It covered 12.5% of the region.
+//
+// Any request whose chunk index landed past 63 - which is every address
+// beyond 256MB - took the `lastChunk >= kMaxPrepareChunks` bail below:
+// prepared at exactly the caller's size, no bit set, uncached, every time
+// it was touched. The allocator can return such an address: allocate()
+// scans pages_ from cursor_ for a free run and wraps at MaxCodePages, and
+// deallocate() pulls cursor_ back to the freed page, so where allocation
+// lands is decided by fragmentation, not confined to a prefix. The
+// measurement that prompted the 1MB -> 4MB raise recorded exactly this
+// shape at the smaller setting: 917 of 1031 requests bypassing the cache
+// with a 64MB ceiling. That raise moved the ceiling from 64MB to 256MB; it
+// did not remove it.
+//
+// Deriving the chunk count from MaxCodeBytesPerProcess removes it: every
+// address the allocator can return now has a bit, the bail is unreachable
+// for in-region addresses, and coalescing stops depending on where the
+// cursor happens to be. At 4MB chunks over 2044MB that is 511 chunks, so
+// eight 64-bit words - 64 bytes of static storage.
+//
+// kPrepareChunkBytes is now a free tuning knob: coverage follows it
+// automatically, so it can be lowered to trade more traps for less wasted
+// preparation per trap (1MB would be 2044 chunks in 32 words) without
+// reintroducing a ceiling. It is left at 4MB here because that is the value
+// this branch has actually measured.
+static constexpr size_t kPrepareChunkBytes = 4 * 1024 * 1024;
+static constexpr size_t kMaxPrepareChunks =
+    (MaxCodeBytesPerProcess + kPrepareChunkBytes - 1) / kPrepareChunkBytes;
+static constexpr size_t kPrepareMaskWords = (kMaxPrepareChunks + 63) / 64;
+static mozilla::Atomic<uint64_t, mozilla::Relaxed>
+    sPreparedChunkMask[kPrepareMaskWords];
+
+// Accessors for the multi-word mask. Each is a single atomic op on one word,
+// so they keep the properties the one-word version relied on: the compound
+// forms stay atomic (a plain load/modify/store would let two compile threads
+// lose one another's bit), and Clear stays async-signal-safe for the SIGBUS
+// handler - array indexing adds no allocation and takes no lock. The array
+// is constant-initialised into .bss, so there is no dynamic initialiser for
+// a signal to race.
+//
+// Every caller is already bounds-checked against kMaxPrepareChunks; the
+// checks are kept at the call sites rather than duplicated here so the two
+// callers that must not silently no-op stay visibly guarded.
+static bool PreparedChunkIsSet(size_t aChunk) {
+  return (sPreparedChunkMask[aChunk / 64] & (uint64_t(1) << (aChunk % 64))) !=
+         0;
+}
+
+static void PreparedChunkSet(size_t aChunk) {
+  sPreparedChunkMask[aChunk / 64] |= uint64_t(1) << (aChunk % 64);
+}
+
+static void PreparedChunkClear(size_t aChunk) {
+  sPreparedChunkMask[aChunk / 64] &= ~(uint64_t(1) << (aChunk % 64));
+}
